# Example .photosignore — copy to $LIBRARY_ROOT/.photosignore and CUT IT DOWN.
#
# This is a menu, not a starting configuration. Every rule below that your library does not
# actually need will be reported as unused on every single run — applied unedited to the
# library this project was built for, five of its seven rules match nothing. Delete the ones
# that do not apply, and add rules when a stray actually shows up in a scan report.
#
# This file is the ONLY source of exclusions. The tool carries no built-in list: not of
# extensions, not of filenames, and not even a rule for dot-files. Without this file, nothing
# whatsoever is excluded.
#
# That matters more than it sounds. A photo-manager's trash directory typically holds real,
# decodable photographs that you deleted — they are indistinguishable from any other JPEG, so
# nothing else will stop them being uploaded, and the storage zone has no undelete.
#
#
# SYNTAX
#
#   *.db              fnmatch glob. No "/", so it matches a file's NAME at any depth.
#   Album/one.psd     Contains "/", so it is anchored to the library root and matches
#                     that one path only.
#   .dtrash/          Trailing "/" means a directory: it is not descended into, so the
#                     files inside are never even enumerated.
#   # comment         Lines starting with # are ignored, as are blank lines.
#
#   Matching ignores case, and ignores Unicode composition — so a pattern typed on a Mac
#   still matches a name stored on Linux.
#
#   There is no negation and no "**". Order therefore never matters: this is a set of rules,
#   not a sequence of them.
#
#   .photosignore always excludes itself; that is the one rule not written here.
#
#
# TWO THINGS WORTH KNOWING
#
#   A rule that matches nothing is reported as unused on every run. That is deliberate — it
#   is the only way a typo like "*.pds" is distinguishable from a rule you simply do not need
#   yet. The practical consequence: write rules when a stray actually appears, rather than
#   defensively up front.
#
#   An ignored file is indistinguishable from an absent one. Adding a rule that matches
#   photos you have already uploaded therefore marks them for removal, which `--prune` will
#   offer to carry out. Broadening a rule is a destructive edit; read the dry-run.


# --- a photo manager's trash: usually real photographs, not junk ------------------
.dtrash/
.Trash/

# --- photo-manager databases, thumbnail caches and sidecars ----------------------
*.db
*.xmp

# --- operating-system litter -----------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- documents that happen to live among the photos ------------------------------
# Prefer anchoring these to exact paths rather than excluding *.psd everywhere, so that a
# new one somewhere else is reported as a stray instead of silently disappearing.
# Some Album/scan-notes.pdf
